# Base-Screen Focus Pollution Warnings

Status: proposed; revised for file-level suite boundaries

## Purpose

Warn when an example or file-level test suite leaves Dwarf Fortress on a
different base-game viewscreen or base-game focus than the state it inherited.

The warnings are nonfatal test-hygiene diagnostics. They expose:

- inter-test pollution caused by per-example setup, the example body, or
  per-example teardown; and
- inter-suite pollution caused anywhere within one spec file, including its
  file-level setup, examples, nested contexts, and file-level teardown.

DwarfSpec-owned components must be removed before the relevant final
observation. Their temporary DFHack screens do not count as base-game focus
changes.

## Terminology and scope

- A suite is one selected spec file during one Busted repeat.
- A later repeat of the same file is a new suite instance with a new baseline.
- Nested `describe`, `context`, `insulate`, and `expose` blocks are naming and
  hook scopes inside their file suite. They do not own independent suite
  pollution guards or emit suite warnings.
- An example is one runnable Busted `it`, `test`, or `spec` case.
- A base-game screen is the topmost non-DFHack viewscreen returned by
  `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true)`.
- Focus state means the focus strings returned for that base-game screen. This
  feature does not compare arbitrary simulation or game data.
- A change warning is a detached `diagnostic.recorded` event whose content has
  warning severity. There is no separate warning-event type.

## Lifecycle and state model

Each file-suite instance owns two observations:

- `S0`, suite entry: captured at Busted file start, before the file body,
  file-level strict setup, lazy setup, nested context code, or examples can
  alter game state.
- `S2`, suite exit: captured at Busted file end, after every example has
  completed, every applicable example teardown has run, file and nested lazy
  teardown has run, file and nested strict teardown has run, DwarfSpec has
  automatically cleaned its resources, and cleanup settlement has completed.

Each runnable example owns two observations:

- `T0`, example entry: captured after all applicable file and nested suite
  setup has run and after DwarfSpec's pre-example reset and settlement, but
  before any project `before_each` hook for that example.
- `T1`, example exit: captured after the example body and `finally` callback,
  after Busted publishes the example result, after every applicable project
  `after_each` hook, and after DwarfSpec's post-example reset, automatic
  unmounting, and settlement.

The comparisons are:

- example pollution: compare `T1` with that example's own `T0`;
- suite pollution: compare `S2` with that file-suite instance's `S0`.

There is no required `S1` prepared-suite observation. Suite setup may
legitimately establish the working state used by its examples. The suite is
clean when all of its teardown restores the inherited `S0` state before `S2`.

Every example receives a fresh `T0`. If one example leaks state, its own
`T1 != T0` comparison attributes the pollution before the next example
inherits that state. A later example starts from the state it actually
received; DwarfSpec does not rewrite its baseline to hide earlier pollution.

A persistent example leak can produce both an example warning and a file-suite
warning. This is intentional: one record identifies the example boundary and
the other proves that the file as a whole failed to restore its inherited
state. Since nested contexts are not suite boundaries, one leak cannot create
a cascade of ancestor-suite warnings.

## Observation and comparison contract

Each observation has two strictly separated parts:

- a private in-process comparison token containing the exact viewscreen state
  and userdata identity; and
- a detached diagnostic record containing only bounded JSON-safe data.

Screen capture distinguishes three outcomes:

- `present`: the call succeeded and returned a base-game viewscreen;
- `none`: the call succeeded and no base-game viewscreen exists; and
- `unavailable`: the call failed and the screen state could not be observed.

`none` is a comparable state, not an observation failure. Therefore:

- `none` compared with `none` is the same screen state;
- `none` compared with `present`, in either direction, is a known change;
- two `present` states compare their exact private userdata identities; and
- any comparison involving `unavailable` has unavailable screen evidence.

A successful `none` observation has an available empty focus set without
calling `getFocusStrings`. A `present` observation queries focus for the exact
captured screen. An `unavailable` screen observation also has unavailable
focus evidence.

The private token:

- exists only until its example or file-suite comparison completes;
- retains exact userdata only when screen capture returned `present`;
- is used only for exact equality comparison; and
- must never enter an event, log line, result, failure, snapshot, or public
  test API.

The detached observation contains:

- screen status;
- a stable screen type label without an address when a screen is present;
- focus status;
- a defensive copy of available focus strings;
- the canonical focus set used for comparison; and
- bounded screen or focus error text when observation failed.

Focus comparison uses canonical set membership. Incidental ordering and
duplicate copies of equivalent focus strings do not count as changes.

Comparison produces independent statuses:

- `screen_comparison`: `EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME`,
  `.CHANGED`, or `.UNAVAILABLE`;
- `focus_comparison`: `EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME`,
  `.CHANGED`, or `.UNAVAILABLE`;
- `details_complete`: whether both dimensions were comparable.

`EBaseScreenFocusComparison` is an immutable numeric enum with `SAME=1`,
`CHANGED=2`, and `UNAVAILABLE=3`; persisted diagnostic records therefore carry
these numeric values instead of repeated status strings.

A change is affirmatively known when:

- both screen observations succeeded and their comparable states differ; or
- both canonical focus sets are available and differ.

When either dimension proves a change, emit one change warning even if the
other dimension is unavailable. The warning must retain every available
before/after detail and explicitly mark incomplete focus or screen evidence.

If neither dimension proves a change but one or both dimensions are
unavailable, publish one non-warning
`base_screen_focus_verification_incomplete` diagnostic. Do not falsely claim
that the state changed.

If both dimensions are available and unchanged, publish nothing.

## Warning timing and attribution

Example warnings contain:

- `scope='example'`;
- `attribution='test'` for an ordinary started example or
  `attribution='before_each'` when setup failed before `test.started`;
- the project-relative suite file identity;
- the retained full example name and source identity when available; and
- the active repeat index.

Busted publishes `test.finished` before any `after_each` hook. Therefore:

- ordinary example warnings appear after `SUCCESS`, `FAILURE`, or `ERROR`;
- `run.current_test` has already been cleared before `T1`; and
- DwarfSpec must retain a separate lifecycle identity until the post-example
  callback consumes it.

If `before_each` fails before Busted publishes `test.started`, `T1` comparison
still runs after the remaining `after_each` hooks and DwarfSpec cleanup.
Publish any warning after the corresponding hook problem output. Use
`attribution='before_each'`, retain the suite identity, leave the unavailable
example identity absent, and never reuse the preceding example's name.

Suite warnings contain:

- `scope='suite'`;
- `attribution='file'`;
- the normalized project-relative spec-file identity; and
- the active repeat index.

A suite warning is published after that file's teardown, DwarfSpec cleanup,
settlement, and `S2` capture, and before the next file begins executing.

## Cleanup and mount exclusions

- `T0` is captured only after `reset('before example')` and its settlement wait
  succeed.
- `T1` is captured only after all project `after_each` hooks,
  `reset('after example')`, and its settlement wait succeed.
- `S2` is captured only after all file and nested lazy and strict teardown,
  `reset('after suite')`, and its settlement wait succeed.
- `reset('after suite')` reuses the existing cleanup registry and marker
  contract. This feature does not introduce a new suite-scoped mount lifetime
  or nested suite cleanup registry.
- Under the existing example-scoped cleanup contract, DwarfSpec resources
  created during suite setup may be drained by the first pre-example reset.
  Resources created during suite teardown are drained by the final suite
  reset.
- Suite entry is not normalized or restored before `S0`; inherited pollution
  is the state the file actually received.
- `getDFViewscreen(true)` resolves beneath temporary DFHack screens, but final
  observations still occur after automatic cleanup.
- Ordinary widgets, overlay widgets, complete `gui.ZScreen` instances, and
  non-owning native attachments do not count merely because they were mounted.
- Input performed through a mount still counts when it changes the underlying
  base-game screen or focus and leaves that change behind.
- If reset or settlement fails, preserve the cleanup error as authoritative,
  clear the affected guard state, and skip the final comparison. Do not emit a
  misleading verification or change diagnostic.
- Emergency abort and generation-loss paths clear unconsumed example and
  file-suite guard state and rely on the existing run cleanup report.

DwarfSpec reports pollution but does not navigate or restore arbitrary
base-game UI state automatically.

## Diagnostic records

Use the existing `diagnostic.recorded` event. Add kind-specific validation for
the new records without changing the existing `command_failure` or operator
diagnostic contracts. DwarfSpec has not released this event contract, so no
schema-version migration or compatibility branch is required.

A change diagnostic has:

```lua
{
    kind='base_screen_focus_changed',
    content={
        severity='warning',
        scope='example' or 'suite',
        attribution='test' or 'before_each' or 'file',
        suite_name='project-relative spec file',
        example_name='full example name', -- ordinary example scope only
        source_identity='project-relative source', -- when available
        repeat_index=1,
        screen_comparison=EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.CHANGED or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.UNAVAILABLE,
        focus_comparison=EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.CHANGED or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.UNAVAILABLE,
        details_complete=true or false,
        before={...},
        after={...},
    },
}
```

An incomplete-verification diagnostic uses the same attribution and
comparison fields:

```lua
{
    kind='base_screen_focus_verification_incomplete',
    content={
        severity='info',
        scope='example' or 'suite',
        attribution='test' or 'before_each' or 'file',
        suite_name='project-relative spec file',
        example_name='full example name', -- when available
        source_identity='project-relative source', -- when available
        repeat_index=1,
        screen_comparison=EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.UNAVAILABLE,
        focus_comparison=EBaseScreenFocusComparison.SAME or
            EBaseScreenFocusComparison.UNAVAILABLE,
        details_complete=false,
        before={...},
        after={...},
    },
}
```

Only detached diagnostic content is communicated. Exact private screen
identity is represented solely by the computed comparison status.

Append exactly one stable `WARNING ...` line to retained run logs from each
change diagnostic and format warning-severity diagnostics in live CLI output.
Incomplete-verification diagnostics remain structured non-warning records and
must not produce a `WARNING` line. Do not add either kind to
`failure_details`.

Warnings and incomplete-verification diagnostics must not change:

- Busted success, failure, error, or pending counts;
- terminal run state;
- process exit status;
- `cleanup_confirmed`;
- mount-cleanup verification; or
- scheduler ownership and recovery behavior.

## Non-goals

- Do not add automatic focus restoration.
- Do not add nested-`describe` suite pollution guards.
- Do not add a warning allowlist, suppression command, or configuration option
  until observed usage demonstrates a need.
- Do not treat DFHack-owned screens as base-game viewscreens.
- Do not broaden the guard to pointer state, map position, selection, pause
  state, simulation state, or arbitrary game data.
- Do not add a new suite-scoped mount lifetime.
- Do not change the thin `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1` wrapper.
- Do not expose a public API for the guard's private observations.

