## Phase 4: Integrate file-suite pollution detection

- [x] Create one active file-suite record at file start and capture `S0`
  before the file body executes.
- [x] Keep nested contexts attached only to example naming and hook execution;
  do not push nested suite records.
- [x] At file end, after all lazy and strict teardown, run
  `reset('after suite')` and its settlement wait.
- [x] Capture `S2` only after suite cleanup succeeds.
- [x] Compare `S2` with `S0` and publish any suite diagnostic.
- [x] Release the complete file-suite record before the next file starts.
- [x] Allow both an example warning and one file-suite warning when pollution
  crosses both boundaries.
- [x] Keep file baselines independent so the next file records the state it
  actually inherited.
- [x] Handle files whose body, setup, teardown, or examples fail, and files
  with no runnable examples, without leaking guard records.
- [x] Clear active file-suite state during emergency abort and generation
  loss.

### Phase 4 tests

- [x] Prove suite setup may change state without an immediate warning when
  suite teardown restores `S0` before `S2`.
- [x] Prove state left by file-body code, strict setup, lazy setup, an example,
  lazy teardown, or strict teardown is visible in the final `S2 != S0`
